  "responses": {
    "concept.arc": "Nonzero linear and angular velocity produce an arc because both velocities are not zero. The linear velocity makes the robot move while the angular velocity makes the robot turn, so when the robot is moving and turning at the same time it causes it to follow a curved path. ",
    "concept.model_limits": "One assumption is that the velocity at which the robot moves may be violated because the real environmental conditions the robot is placed in could cause its speed or movement to differ from the commanded value. \n",
    "concept.velocity_pose": "A velocity command requires a duration to predict the robot's pose because, without a duration, we would not be able to determine the robot's location in the x and y coordinates. It also helps us calculate how far the robot has traveled and determine its new position. ",
    "environment.issue": "jjjjj",
    "environment.mode": "live",
