    "mission_1.compare.arc": "My prediction for the final orientation in radians was correct at 1.6 rad. The only difference was the path length. I predicted a path length of 0.60 m, but the actual path length was 0.457 m. One possible cause could be that the robot\u2019s actual speed was lower than the expected 0.15 m/s. Evidence such as the measured velocity and odometry data during the trial would help determine if the robot actually traveled at the expected speed.",
    "mission_1.compare.straight": "My prediction was accurate for the x coordinate, but the measured path length was different. I predicted a path length of 0.45 m, while the robot traveled 0.319 m. One possible cause could be that the robot's actual speed was lower than the expected 0.15 m/s. Evidence such as the measured velocity and odometry data during the trial would help determine if the robot actually traveled at the expected speed.",
    "mission_1.compare.turn_then_drive": "My prediction for the final x and y coordinates and the orientation in radians was correct. The only difference was the path length. I predicted a path length of 0.30 m, but the actual path length was 0.214 m. One possible cause could be that the robot\u2019s actual speed was lower than the expected 0.15 m/s. Evidence such as the measured velocity and odometry data during the trial would help determine if the robot actually traveled at the expected speed.",
    "mission_1.error.arc": "",
    "mission_1.error.straight": "",
    "mission_1.error.turn_then_drive": "",
    "mission_1.error_source": "It was probably a measurement or localization error because my predictions were accurate based on the given linear and angular velocities, but the observed motion remained at approximately zero. ",
    "mission_1.hallway_analysis": "I would choose the arc motion because the robot is moving and turning at the same time, which could make a person nearby uncomfortable. I would reduce the robot\u2019s speed so its movement is slower and easier to predict. The tradeoff is that the robot would take longer to complete the movement. One way to measure if the motion is acceptable would be to make sure the robot keeps a safe distance from a person and test if it stays at that distance while moving.",
    "mission_1.largest_error": "They all had discrepancies, but the Arc sequence had the largest discrepancy, with a position error of 0.538 m and a heading error of 1.60 rad. ",
    "mission_1.model_vs_observation": "My predicted results were different from the observed motion. My predictions were all nonzero, while the observed results were all approximately zero. \n",
